.\" The manual page for linuxzpl.py. Read it with: man ./help/linuxzpl.1
.\" tests/test_help.py checks that it names every option linuxzpl.py takes,
.\" and that groff finds nothing to warn about in it.
.TH LINUXZPL 1 2026-11-05 "LinuxZPL 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.\" Identifiers such as KeyError and --save-file are not to be split
.nh
.SH NAME
linuxzpl \- design Zebra ZPL labels, and fill and save ZPL templates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linuxzpl.py
[\fB\-\-gtk\fR | \fB\-\-qt\fR]
[\fB\-\-load\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
[\fB\-\-field\fR \fIN=DATA\fR]...]
.br
.B linuxzpl.py
\fB\-\-load\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
[\fB\-\-field\fR \fIN=DATA\fR]...
\fB\-\-save\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B linuxzpl.py
[\fB\-\-gtk\fR | \fB\-\-qt\fR]
\fB\-\-show\-help\fR [\fITOPIC\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
LinuxZPL is a visual designer for labels in ZPL, the language of Zebra
thermal printers.
It prints them straight to a Zebra on the network, with no printing system
in between.
Everything on its canvas is measured in printer dots, and drawn as the
printer will print it.
.PP
It has two frontends that behave the same and write the same ZPL: GTK 3
(PyGObject) and Qt 5 (PySide2).
With neither \fB\-\-gtk\fR nor \fB\-\-qt\fR, GTK is used when it is
installed and Qt when it is not.
.PP
With \fB\-\-save\-file\fR, no window opens and no GUI toolkit is needed: the
template is filled in with the \fB\-\-field\fR data and written out, for use
from a script.
.PP
The designer's own help is under \fBHelp \(-> Contents\fR (F1).
It opens in Yelp, or where Yelp is not installed, in LinuxZPL's own help
viewer, which \fB\-\-show\-help\fR opens alone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-gtk\fR
Use the GTK 3 frontend.
If PyGObject is not installed, says what to install and exits with status 1.
.TP
\fB\-\-qt\fR
Use the Qt 5 frontend.
If PySide2 is not installed, says what to install and exits with status 1.
Not together with \fB\-\-gtk\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-load\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Open the ZPL template \fIFILE\fR, in the designer or for
\fB\-\-save\-file\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-field\fR \fIN=DATA\fR
Give the template's \fB^FN\fR field \fIN\fR, from 1 to 9999, the data
\fIDATA\fR.
\fIDATA\fR is everything after the first \fB=\fR, so it may hold an
\fB=\fR of its own.
May be given more than once; a later one for the same \fIN\fR wins.
Needs \fB\-\-load\-file\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-save\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Write the template, with any \fB\-\-field\fR data filled in, to \fIFILE\fR,
and exit without opening a window.
Anything in the template the file cannot keep is named on standard error.
Needs \fB\-\-load\-file\fR, and is not taken with \fB\-\-gtk\fR or
\fB\-\-qt\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-help\fR [\fITOPIC\fR]
Open only the help, in LinuxZPL's own viewer, without the designer.
\fITOPIC\fR is a page of the help, by the name of its file in
\fBhelp/C\fR without \fB.page\fR, or a section of one,
\fIpage\fR\fB#\fR\fIsection\fR; without it, the viewer opens at the
contents.
Topics written to standard input, one to a line, open in the same window,
which is how the designer sends it the help asked for next.
Not taken with \fB\-\-load\-file\fR, \fB\-\-field\fR or
\fB\-\-save\-file\fR.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show a summary of the options and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: the window was closed, or \fB\-\-save\-file\fR wrote its file.
.TP
.B 1
The template could not be read or saved; a \fB\-\-field\fR was not
\fIN=DATA\fR; \fB\-\-field\fR or \fB\-\-save\-file\fR came without
\fB\-\-load\-file\fR, or \fB\-\-save\-file\fR with \fB\-\-gtk\fR or
\fB\-\-qt\fR; \fB\-\-show\-help\fR named no page of the help, or came
with a template; or the GUI toolkit needed is not installed.
The message on standard error says which.
.TP
.B 2
An option was not recognised or was missing its value, or \fB\-\-gtk\fR
and \fB\-\-qt\fR were both given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open a template in the designer:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
linuxzpl.py \-\-load\-file label.zpl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fill ^FN1 and ^FN2 and write the label to out.zpl, with no window:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
linuxzpl.py \-\-load\-file label.zpl \-\-field 1=Hello \e
    \-\-field 2=World \-\-save\-file out.zpl
.fi
.RE
.SH PYTHON API
The same filling-in is open to a Python program, with no GUI toolkit:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
from zplcore import Label

label = Label.load("shipping.zpl")   # or Label.from_zpl(text)
label[1] = "Hello"                   # ^FN1
label.fill({2: "World"}, f3="123")   # several at once
label["customer"] = "ACME"           # the field whose ^FX ID is customer
label.copies = 5                     # ^PQ
label.save("out.zpl")                # what \-\-save\-file writes
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBLabel.load\fR(\fIpath\fR) and \fBLabel.from_zpl\fR(\fItext\fR) read a
template.
A field is set by its \fB^FN\fR number, or by an ID given to it with a
\fB^FXid:\fR\fIname\fR comment inside the field; \fBids\fR lists the IDs.
\fBset_data\fR sets a field that has neither, by its data or its place,
and \fBserial\fR makes one a \fB^SN\fR serial number.
\fBto_zpl\fR(\fItemplate\fR=False) gives the ZPL, \fBrender\fR() a PIL
image, \fBsave\fR(\fIpath\fR) and \fBsave_image\fR(\fIpath\fR) write them,
and \fBdropped\fR lists what a save would not keep.
The template itself is never changed, so one \fBLabel\fR can be filled again
and again.
Bad input raises ValueError, KeyError or IndexError; a file that cannot be
read raises OSError.
README.md, beside linuxzpl.py, has the whole of it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/linuxzpl/settings.ini
The printer (its address and port, or a USB printer's serial number) and
its resolution, the label size, the window's
size and place, the files under File \(-> Open Recent, the grid, and the
help viewer's text size.
Written as \fIsettings.ini\fR beside linuxzpl.py instead when the config
directory refuses writes.
.TP
.I help/C/
Beside linuxzpl.py: the designer's help, as Mallard pages, shown in
.BR yelp (1)
or, without it, in LinuxZPL's own viewer.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/linuxzpl/help/
The help made into PDF, for the help viewer's Print.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the settings file goes, in place of \fI~/.config\fR.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where the help's PDFs go, in place of \fI~/.cache\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yelp (1),
.BR xdg\-open (1)
